# Files Next.js 16 writes INTO web/ that must never be tracked.
#
# `next dev` / `next build` regenerate all three on every run, so a tracked or
# unignored copy shows up as untracked/modified in `git status --short` before a
# worker has written a line of code — the same class as the tsbuildinfo churn
# (#661, ignored in the repo-root .gitignore).
#
# /AGENTS.md and /CLAUDE.md — the managed AI-agent-rules scaffold, written by
#   next/dist/server/lib/generate-agent-files.js (called from start-server.js ->
#   ensureAgentRulesForDev) whenever an AI coding agent is detected in the
#   environment. This repo disables the writer with `agentRules: false` in
#   next.config.mjs (next's documented opt-out); these two lines are the belt to
#   that braces, so a future Next that drops or renames the switch still cannot
#   dirty the tree.
#
# /next-env.d.ts — Next's own generated type reference; `next dev` points it at
#   .next/dev/types and `next build` at .next/types, so while it is tracked the
#   two commands flip it back and forth forever. Next documents the fix the same
#   way ("Add it to .gitignore. If your project already tracks the file, remove
#   it from Git" — node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/05-config/
#   02-typescript.md). It is regenerated before the type check in every
#   `next dev` / `next build`, so nothing needs the committed copy — verified by
#   running a full `npm run build` with the file absent (exit 0).
#
# Guard: web/src/lib/nextManagedFiles.test.ts refuses a tree that drops either
# half of this (the ignore rules, or `agentRules: false`).
/AGENTS.md
/CLAUDE.md
/next-env.d.ts
